def is_convex_polygon(points):
    def cross(a, b, c):
        """Return the z-component of the cross product of ab and ac."""
        return (b[0] - a[0]) * (c[1] - a[1]) - (b[1] - a[1]) * (c[0] - a[0])

    n = len(points)
    if n < 3:
        return False  # A polygon must have at least three vertices
    if n == 3:
        return True  # All triangles are convex

    sign = None
    for i in range(n):
        a, b, c = points[i], points[(i + 1) % n], points[(i + 2) % n]
        current = cross(a, b, c)
        if current == 0:
            return False  # Vertices are collinear
        if i == 0:
            sign = 1 if current > 0 else -1
        elif sign * current < 0:
            return False  # Change of turn direction
    return True
```

The function checks whether the directions of right turns at consecutive triples of vertices are all the same, and also rules out collinear vertices since a polygon with three collinear points has no interior.

It marks the sign of the z-component of a right turn from vertex pairs i 1, i 2 and i 2, i 3, computed using the cross-product formula. After three or more vertices, it looks at the cross product coming from every vertex, moves the window along by one at a time, and wraps around from the last vertex back to the first. A zero cross product means three points lie on a straight line, so the function returns False immediately. After completing the loop, it returns True, meaning all previous cross products keep the window turning in the same direction. Two adjacent collinear pairs that are not aligned give a zero product at their shared vertex, and a triangle concave at its first vertex has a final window whose turn points towards the interior rather than away. The letter N, revisited, should therefore describe a convex polygon and a C shape, also described with collinear pairs, should not.

