from copy import deepcopy
from collections import deque

def flood_fill(grid, sr, sc, new_value):
    if not grid or sr < 0 or sc < 0 or sr >= len(grid) or sc >= len(grid[0]):
        return deepcopy(grid)  # Not a cell, so the grid is unchanged
    original = grid[sr][sc]
    if original == new_value:
        return deepcopy(grid)  # Same value, so the grid is unchanged
    result = deepcopy(grid)
    queue = deque([(sr, sc)])
    result[sr][sc] = new_value
    while queue:
        r, c = queue.popleft()
        for dr, dc in [(-1, 0), (1, 0), (0, -1), (0, 1)]:
            nr, nc = r + dr, c + dc
            if 0 <= nr < len(grid) and 0 <= nc < len(grid[0]) and result[nr][nc] == original:
                result[nr][nc] = new_value
                queue.append((nr, nc))
    return result
```

How the flood fills behave is specified by three rules. Cells are reachable only by moving horizontally or vertically, so diagonal and out-of-bounds cells are ignored. Cells change only when they had the same initial value as the starting cell, so the fill stops at cells of a different value and at cells already visited. If the new value equals the starting value, the cell is left alone and every copy goes through unchanged.

The implementation performs an on-grid breadth-first visit, which leaves the input untouched and avoids infinite loops. First it makes a full deep copy of the grid so that the caller's data cannot be modified. If the starting index is not a valid row and column number, or if the new value equals the starting value, it returns the copy at once. Otherwise it looks up the original value through the copy and records the starting cell's coordinates in a queue. It marks cells in the queue as new and adds adjacent cells as it processes them, stopping when a cell of any other value or a visited cell comes up. Because the copy is used throughout, whatever grid the caller supplies outside the function is not changed.

