To avoid false positives, I first skip vertices where a neighboring point shares both its x and y coordinates, since three collinear vertices can make an otherwise convex polygon collapse. Then I record a running sign for the cross-product at each consecutive triple of points, switching direction on a sign change and returning False in that case. Three vertices all lying on the same line make every cross-product zero, so the loop skips past them and yields True, which is the intended rule. The code uses only integer addition and multiplication.

