There are three kinds of command: appending a value, undoing, and redoing. The document starts empty, and after every append a permanent stack of undone values is emptied. Undoing, when the document is not already empty, moves the final value to that stack. Redoing, when the stack is not empty, takes its top value and appends it back to the document.

Keeping a separate undo stack is what makes redoing safe. An append clears the stack so nothing can be redone behind it. Only the last undone value can be recovered. When the stack is empty there is nothing to redo, and that means nothing is added.

